// Jade has no `exit`, and until v1.3.20 this file compiled clean.
//
// A single `use` — any `use`, this one included — turned off the
// undefined-variable check for the whole file, on the theory that an import
// might be what defines the name. `exit` is not defined by anything, so the
// call lowered to a read of a nil global, and `jade build` printed
// `built: ./prog` for a program that then died in the runtime with no message.
//
// The check now stays on for a stdlib import, which registers everything it
// contributes and therefore leaves nothing unaccounted for. Leniency is kept
// only for imports that really are opaque to type inference — a user module or
// a `from … use` — and those are caught after inlining instead (see
// `codegen::check_globals_bound`).
//
// What to write instead is in the error message: an uncaught top-level `raise`
// exits 1, and that is the whole exit-code mechanism the language has.
use std::env

fn main() {
    print(f"running as {env.args()[0]}")
    return 0
}

exit(main())
